WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.840
Democratic front-runner Joe Biden
recently sketched his education agenda for 2020.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.680
He promises to triple federal spending on
districts with large numbers of low-income

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:18.480
students, double the number of health professionals
in schools, provide universal pre-K, embrace

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.160
race-based policies, and even “defeat”
the NRA.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.890
There’s not a mention of accountability
or of charter schools.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:30.090
This would be the most liberal
education agenda in American history.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:34.950
But remarkably, compared to his presidential
rivals, Biden’s plan looks centrist, and

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:35.950
rather modest.

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:38.640
Key Democratic rivals have promised hundreds

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.290
of billions in new education spending.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:45.050
Both parties have long wooed the center when
it came to education.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:49.820
It’s how Republicans showed their soft side
and Democrats like President Obama showed

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.410
their reformist bona fides.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:56.250
But, today, President Trump oversees arguably
the most conservative Department of Education

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.320
in history, and Democrats have responded in
kind.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:06.340
Whoever wins in November 2020, federal education
policy looks set to be an increasingly partisan affair.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.720
Do you think education's going
 to be a partisan issue in 2020?

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.860
Let us know in our poll. Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:22.340
in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

